<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What was Your Previous School Like?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Show comprehension of the main plot and characters in short stories. Reading: Follow instructions for academic tasks or projects. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by reading base words and inflectional endings (-s, -es, -ed, -ing).
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify the main plot and characters in short stories about changing schools and making new friends (e.g., "In the story, Gianluigi and Emma felt nervous about their first day at a new school, but they made new friends during lunch.").
Can follow written instructions to complete school-related tasks or projects (e.g., "Step 1: Interview a classmate about their previous school. Step 2: Write three sentences comparing your schools.").
Can recognize base words and inflectional endings (-s, -es, -ed, -ing) in texts about classmates and school activities (e.g., "Giacomo talks to his classmates every day." "Giuseppe played soccer after school." "Giulietta is studying for the test.").
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays two large pictures on the board: one of a small, old-fashioned school in the countryside and another of a large, modern school in the city. Students are asked to briefly think about what they see. Then, in pairs, they discuss which school looks more interesting or what activities they think happen in each. (e.g., "The old school looks quiet." "The new school is bigger than the old one.") This activates vocabulary from Lesson 1 like 'big,' 'small,' 'old,' 'new,' 'quiet,' 'noisy,' 'countryside,' 'city,' 'activity.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a text for key information. For example, the teacher presents a short paragraph (2-3 sentences) about a fictional student, Camila, describing her previous school. The teacher reads it aloud, highlighting how to look for names, places, and descriptive adjectives. (e.g., "Camila used to go to a small school in the countryside. It was very friendly.") The teacher also models the pronunciation of words that might have inflectional endings, like "walks" or "played," explicitly stating the sounds /s/ and /d/ without overemphasizing them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary from the previous lesson and the pre-task discussion.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'nervous' mean? Is it a good feeling or a bad feeling?" (Expected: A bad feeling, worried.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a school is 'modern,' is it old or new?" (Expected: New.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'he plays,' what sound do you hear at the end of 'plays'?" (Expected: /z/ sound.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (approximately 100-120 words) titled "Our New Classmates: Marco and Elena." The text describes two new students, Marco and Elena, their feelings about starting a new school, and brief details about their previous schools and hometowns. It includes vocabulary such as 'nervous,' 'excited,' 'friendly,' 'classmates,' 'library,' 'playground,' 'activities,' 'used to,' 'moved from,' 'bigger,' 'smaller.' It uses Past Simple to describe their previous schools and Present Simple for their current feelings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of the main plot and characters in short stories.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the story silently for the first time. Then, the teacher asks: "Who are the new classmates in the story?" and "What is the story mainly about?" (Expected: Two new students, Marco and Elena, and their first day at a new school.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the text again to verify specific details by marking sentences as True or False.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Marco felt excited on his first day. (False - nervous)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Elena used to study in a very big school. (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Marco's old school had a small playground. (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. They made new friends during recess. (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases related to Marco and Elena based on the text. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Match: a) Marco's previous school... b) Elena's previous school...</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">With: i) was bigger than her new one. ii) had a small library.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying grammar (Present/Past Simple, Comparatives), recognizing vocabulary (classmate, school, library, recess, activities, nervous, friendly, big, small, used to), and applying phonemic awareness to base words and inflectional endings (-s, -ed, -ing) in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Vocabulary and Grammar Focus (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the story, but with key vocabulary words or verb forms missing. They choose the correct word from a box (e.g., 'nervous', 'excited', 'bigger', 'smaller', 'used to', 'played'). Additionally, some sentences require students to underline the base word and circle the inflectional ending (e.g., "Marco **plays** soccer." Base: play, Ending: -s). This reinforces phonemic awareness by recognizing these forms in writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Sentence Analysis (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> Students re-read specific sentences from the text. They are asked to identify sentences that use Past Simple (e.g., "Elena used to walk to school."), Present Simple (e.g., "Our new school is friendly."), and Comparatives (e.g., "Her old school was bigger than the new one."). The teacher guides them to underline the verb forms and comparative adjectives, providing sentence frames if needed (e.g., "The verb '______' is in the Past Simple."). Students also identify words with plural '-s' endings or third-person singular '-s' endings and categorize them by their sound (/s/, /z/, /ɪz/). Example: 'friends' (/z/), 'books' (/s/), 'watches' (/ɪz/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Reading Instructions and Peer Check:</b> The teacher provides short, simple written instructions for a classroom activity (e.g., "Step 1: In pairs, compare two activities from the story. Step 2: Write one sentence about each activity. Step 3: Read your sentences to another pair."). Students read the instructions individually, then work in pairs to rephrase them in their own words to check comprehension. This specifically targets the objective "Reading: Follow instructions for academic tasks or projects."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Information Transfer (Group Project Outline).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a printed "Classmate Profile Card" for two *other* fictional new classmates (e.g., Sofia and Carlos), similar in style to the story read in Stage 2. These cards include details about their previous schools, subjects they liked, favorite activities, and feelings (e.g., "Sofia moved from Chiriquí. Her old school had a very modern library. Carlos used to play soccer every day after school.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The groups' task is to read the two new classmate profiles and complete a comparison chart based on the information. The chart has columns for "Name," "Previous School Description (Past Simple)," "Favorite Activity (Present Simple/Used to)," and "Comparison (Comparatives)."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example chart questions:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was Sofia's previous school like?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who has a more interesting hobby, Sofia or Carlos?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which school was smaller, Sofia's or Carlos's?"</li>
                </ul>
                Groups must collaboratively read, extract information, and then write short answers in the chart, making sure to use Past Simple, Present Simple, and Comparatives as required. This activity directly links to the learning outcome of identifying main plot/characters in short stories and following instructions for academic tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for reading comprehension and a quick individual exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a new short paragraph (3-4 sentences) about a fictional student's previous school or a set of 3 written instructions for a simple drawing activity (e.g., "Draw a small library. Add three books. Color the books green and blue.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They individually answer two comprehension questions about the paragraph (e.g., "Where was the student's old school?" "What was one activity they used to do?") OR complete the drawing activity based on the instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Additionally, they identify one word in the text with an '-s' ending and write its base form and the type of ending (plural or 3rd person singular).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy in answering comprehension questions (identifying main plot/details).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow written instructions correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of base words and inflectional endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'school,' 'activity,' 'library,' 'used to') in responses.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short reflection:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I understood well in the story today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was easy/difficult for me to follow the instructions because..."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies helped me understand the stories today (e.g., looking for keywords, re-reading)?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about new classmates did I find most interesting?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading comprehension strategies worked well for the students (e.g., gist reading, scanning for details, graphic organizers)? Did students effectively identify main plots and characters?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the instructions for academic tasks clear enough for most students to follow? What adjustments are needed for future instruction on following written directions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did students recognize base words and inflectional endings in the texts? Is more focused practice needed for phonemic awareness in reading?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which grammar points (Past Simple, Comparatives) and vocabulary items were most challenging for students to understand in the written texts?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the information read today about their new classmates and their previous schools to engage in guided discussions and peer interviews. They will practice asking and answering questions to compare their own school experiences with those of their 'new classmates' (fictional or real), building on the vocabulary and grammar practiced through reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>